const title = "LG Dryer D80, D90, D95 Error: What It Means — Same Day";
const description = "LG dryer showing D80, D90, or D95? It's the Flow Sense airflow warning. What each code means, how to clear it yourself, and when the sensor is the real problem.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What does the D80 code mean on an LG dryer?",
    a: "D80 is part of LG's Flow Sense system, a sensor in the exhaust that watches airflow. D80 means the dryer measured roughly 80% blockage in the venting somewhere between the machine and the outside vent. It is a warning, not a breakdown: the dryer is telling you the exhaust is badly restricted, which makes drying slow and, left alone, is a fire risk. The fix is almost always cleaning the lint filter and the vent, not replacing a part."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between D80, D90, and D95?",
    a: "They are the same warning at rising severity. D80 is about 80% airflow restriction, D90 is about 90%, and D95 is about 95%. At D95 the airflow is so choked that the dryer will not resume normal operation until the blockage is cleared. All three point to the same root cause, a restricted exhaust path, and all three are cleared the same way."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I clear the D80 code?",
    a: "Clean the airflow path end to end. Pull and clean the lint filter (and wash it if it has fabric-softener film), then disconnect and clear the flexible vent hose behind the dryer, and finally check that the exterior vent flap opens freely and isn't packed with lint or blocked by a bird nest. Restart a cycle. If the path is genuinely clear, the code should not come back. LG dryers clear the warning on their own once airflow is restored."
  },
  {
    q: "The vent is clean but I still get D80 or D90. Now what?",
    a: "When the entire vent path is verified clear and the code still shows, the problem has shifted from a blockage to the machine itself. A failing airflow sensor or a weak high-limit thermostat can send false restriction readings to the control board. At that point it is a diagnostic and part replacement rather than a cleaning, and it is where our techs take over. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a D80 code dangerous?",
    a: "The code itself is a safety feature doing its job. What it warns about, a heavily restricted dryer vent, is genuinely dangerous, because trapped lint and heat are a leading cause of dryer fires. That is why it is worth acting on the same day rather than ignoring it. Clear the vent; if the code persists, get the sensor checked."
  }
];

