<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/lg-d80-error"
  eyebrow="LG Dryer D80 / D90 / D95 · Flow Sense Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Dryer D80, D90, D95: What the Code Means and How to Clear It"
  subtitle="These are LG's Flow Sense airflow warnings. Here's what each one means, how to clear it safely, and when the sensor (not the vent) is the real fault."
  heroImageTitle="LG Dryer D80 D90 D95 Error"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">D80, D90, and D95 are airflow warnings, not breakdowns.</h2>

    <p><strong>The D80, D90, and D95 codes on an LG dryer come from its Flow Sense system, a sensor in the exhaust that measures how freely air is leaving the machine. D80 means about 80% blockage in the vent, D90 about 90%, and D95 about 95%, at which point the dryer won't run normally until airflow is restored. All three mean the same thing: the exhaust path is choked with lint. Ninety percent of the time the fix is cleaning the lint filter and the vent, and the code clears itself. Only when the vent is verified clear and the code persists is it a sensor problem. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>People see a code on the display and assume the control board or a heating part has failed. On these three codes that is rarely true. The dryer is not broken, it is protecting itself, because a blocked vent is both why your clothes take three cycles to dry and a genuine fire hazard. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair get these calls constantly, and most of them never needed a service visit at all, just a proper vent cleaning.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHAT EACH MEANS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · What each code means</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same warning, rising severity.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>D80 — roughly 80% airflow restriction</h3>
      <p>The first and most common warning. The dryer still runs, but drying is slow and the sensor is flagging that the vent needs attention. Treat it as an early nudge to clean.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>D90 — roughly 90% airflow restriction</h3>
      <p>The blockage has worsened. Drying times are noticeably long, the machine may run hot, and the exhaust is close to fully choked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>D95 — roughly 95% airflow restriction</h3>
      <p>The most severe of the three. Airflow is so restricted that the dryer will not return to normal operation until the path is cleared. At this point cleaning is not optional.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Because all three share one root cause, a restricted exhaust, they share one fix. You do not diagnose them differently; you clear the vent and the number takes care of itself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · How to clear it yourself</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean the airflow path end to end.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter.</strong> Pull it and clear the lint. If it has a waxy film from fabric softener, wash it with soap and water and let it dry, because that film chokes airflow even when it looks clean.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the vent hose.</strong> Unplug the dryer, pull it away from the wall, disconnect the flexible hose, and remove the lint packed inside it and at both connections. This is where most of the blockage hides.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the exterior vent.</strong> Go outside and confirm the vent flap opens when the dryer runs. Clear any lint mat, and check for a bird or rodent nest, which is common on ground-floor and low roof-line vents.</li>
      <li><strong>Run a cycle.</strong> With the path clear, start a normal cycle. The code should not return. LG dryers clear the Flow Sense warning automatically once airflow is restored.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> cleaning the filter, hose, and exterior vent is safe homeowner work and it fixes the large majority of these codes. Do not open the dryer cabinet to test the sensor or the heating components yourself, and never run a gas dryer with the gas line disturbed. If the code survives a full cleaning, that's the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 WHEN TO CALL -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · When it's not the vent</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A clean vent and a persistent code means the sensor.</h2>

    <p>When the entire exhaust path is verified clear and D80 or D90 keeps coming back, the fault has moved inside the machine. A failing airflow sensor or a weakened high-limit thermostat can report a restriction that isn't there, tripping the code on clean airflow. Diagnosing that means testing the sensor and thermostat against spec and replacing the failed part, which is a tech job, not a cleaning.</p>

    <p>Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair carry the common LG airflow sensors and thermostats, so a false-reading Flow Sense fault is usually a one-visit fix. We verify the vent ourselves first (no point replacing a sensor that was reading a real blockage), then test the circuit before quoting. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong> Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, Orange, and Ventura in most cases.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/dryer-repair/">dryer repair</a> hub, LG-specific coverage on <a href="/brands/lg-dryer-repair/">LG dryer repair</a>, and if your dryer runs but clothes stay wet, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a>. A chronically restricted vent is also worth a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Nearby: <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a>. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG D80 / D90 / D95: common questions.</h2>

